  "text": ".dequeueReusableCell<0./b6634ffa-b094-4316-9840-03bdfb14d4e7.webp>The question requires counting the different triangles whose vertices are all the vertices in a regular octagon. Since a octagon can only have a triangle when three vertices touch three sides, 2 adjacent sides with one diagonal connects the opposite vertices of the adjacent sides which forms the triangle. To find the total number of triangles, we determine the number of diagonals in an octagon to connect the non-adjacent vertices. This is found to be 5 diagonals (noting that for the full equation of n vertices, length would n-3 diagonals). Since each diagonal forms 2 triangles, the total number of triangles possible is 5*2 = 10, however, this counts every triangle 2 times (those formed by the vertex in the middle and the side) so the number of distinct triangles would just be (10-3)-(3-1)/2 = 27. This is the maximum value possible for any triangle with an integer side lengths in any regular polygon. Hence, the final answer is (27).",
